The bud:// protocol handler.

Before opening a channel the scheme asks the `budscan` core, and **only
verified bytes** are written into the channel. An answer that does not verify
is turned into an error page; under no circumstance do the bytes reach the
parser. Bytes whose route was not proven (`claim only`, `transport only`) are
shown with that badge, under an opaque principal rather than the name's own
origin: an unproven resolution must not borrow what the name's verified pages
stored or sent.

The C ABI the C++ calls is declared in `budscan_ffi.h`, added by this patch
next to the two classes; the Rust library behind it is `budscan_ffi`.

Why this is not a redirect: if `bud://ayaz.bud` were redirected to an HTTPS
gateway, the origin of the page would be the origin of the gateway, and two
different names under `bud://` would share one origin. Opening the channel
ourselves keeps the origin at `bud://<name>`.

